Read a flight log and print its readings, with no design file

Usage: hpr analyze [OPTIONS] <LOG> [MORE_LOGS]...

Arguments:
  <LOG>
          The flight log: a file in any format HPR Sim reads, or another logger's file read by --mapping or --layout

  [MORE_LOGS]...
          More logs of the same flight: each is read by its format's own reader, and all go on one timeline by a shared liftoff, read side by side and never averaged

Options:
      --json
          Print one JSON document on standard output instead of text, errors included

      --stages
          The logs are of a staged flight, one logger to a stage, all leaving the pad together: no spread is reported, their readings being of different bodies

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use color. With auto, output to a terminal is colored, and NO_COLOR, FORCE_COLOR and CLICOLOR_FORCE are followed. JSON output never has color
          
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, always, never]

      --mapping <FILE>
          Read the log as a table, by this column mapping: a JSON file, as --print-mapping prints one (schema/cli/analyze-mapping.schema.json)

      --layout <FILE>
          Read the log as binary records, or a serial console's capture of them, by this record layout: a JSON file (schema/cli/analyze-layout.schema.json)

      --sheet <N>
          Which sheet of a workbook (.xlsx or .xls) to read, counting from 0. Without it, the one sheet that reads as a log is read. It works with the format's own reader and with --mapping, --print-mapping and --fip-header

      --recording <N>
          Which recording to read from a file that holds several, such as an Entacore AIM raw dump, counting from 0

      --fip-header <FILE>
          Read a Featherweight Raven's FIP export pasted into a workbook without its header line. This JSON file states the missing header (schema/cli/analyze-fip-header.schema.json)

      --print-mapping
          Print what the table's header states, as a column mapping to edit and give back with --mapping, and read nothing more. The header must name a clock with a unit of time, such as `Time (s)`

      --record
          Print every channel of the log in SI units (degrees for angles), as one JSON document (schema/cli/analyze-record.schema.json), in place of the readings

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Examples:
  Read a PerfectFlite log's apogee, top speed and timeline:
      hpr analyze flight.pf2
  Read two logs of one flight side by side, on one timeline:
      hpr analyze telemetrum.csv telemega.csv
  Print what your own logger's table header states, as a mapping to edit:
      hpr analyze flight.csv --print-mapping > logger.json
  Read the table by the edited mapping, and print every channel it holds:
      hpr analyze flight.csv --mapping logger.json --record

Guide: https://hpr.fusionspace.co/cli.html#hpr-analyze
